{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>Whereby shall I know that I shall inherit it?</b> Our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said (Nedarim 32a) that because Abram said 'Whereby shall I know,' he was punished with 'You shall surely know' — and reason refuses to accept this derash, that his descendants should suffer so great a punishment on account of the sign that Abram asked for, while Abram himself received no punishment at all and his descendants' teeth would be set on edge. Therefore my heart tells me and completes the matter that the Egyptian exile had other causes, and our Rabbis, of blessed memory, disagreed about it — and you will find all the opinions in the Maharsha, for his hand gathered them; it is too lengthy to bring upon it all those opinions. But the difficulty for the author of this midrash is this: whatever the reason for the exile may be, why did the Holy One, blessed be He, announce this evil tidings to Abram, to distress him for nothing? To this he said that on account of the sin of 'Whereby shall I know' — for he wanted to know something he had no need to know, for what business did he have to ask for a sign concerning the word of HaShem? — therefore he was punished with 'You shall surely know,' that the Holy One, blessed be He, made known to him a thing to distress him. And this too is measure for measure." "And regarding this question that Abram asked, many have wondered why he asked for a sign concerning the inheritance of the land but did not ask for a sign concerning the promise of seed. And I ask yet one more question: why did he not ask for a sign concerning the land immediately, when the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him the first time, 'To your seed I will give this land' etc. (Genesis 12:7)? On these questions too the opinions have multiplied. And I say that the sign which he asked for was not because Abram had any doubt about the promise of El, blessed be He — rather, he wanted the Holy One, blessed be He, to cut a covenant with him, to remove from him every claimant and disputant. For above, He said to him, 'To your seed I will give' — this implies an outright gift, and about this Abram had no need to request the cutting of a covenant, for who would contest the gift that HaShem gave him, for the world and its fullness are His, blessed be He, and it is in His hand to give the land to whomever He wishes. But after the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him, 'to give you this land to inherit it' (Genesis 15:7) — this implies that this giving that He mentioned was the law of inheritance. For Noah, when he divided the earth among his sons, gave the land of Canaan to the sons of Shem, and now he was coming into it by way of inheritance. Then Abram thought that perhaps the other heirs of Shem would contest their portion, for the sons of Shem were Elam and Arphaxad and Asshur and Lud and Aram (Genesis 10:22), and Abram descended from Arphaxad — so perhaps the other sons of Shem would contest the inheritance. Therefore he said, 'Whereby shall I know that I shall inherit it?' (Genesis 15:8) — what is the sign that I alone am the heir without contest? And the answer came to him: 'Take for me a heifer of three years old' etc. (Genesis 15:9). Just as HaShem cut a covenant of eternal salt with Aaron (Numbers 18:19) to remove from him the contest of Korah, so HaShem cut a covenant with Abram to remove from him every claimant and disputant — for this was the established ordinance of former times, that all who cut a covenant would pass between the pieces, as is known."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {1 "I read רַבּוּ הַדֵּעוֹת as 'the opinions multiplied' rather than 'the authorities multiplied,' following the context of multiple answers to these questions."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:28:23.857543427Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}